userStories.description:
**Goal** — Add a standalone structured MCP identity extractor and conflict-preserving own-tool ledger for Codex and Cursor reports.

**Motivation** — The structured `rawInput` fields are set by the adapter's MCP routing.

**Approach** — New `src/client/own-tool-calls.ts`: `mcpCallIdentityOf` reads the structured `rawInput` of a `tool_call` or `tool_call_update` (either update type, for either kind) and `createOwnToolLedger` records identities per `toolCallId`.

The identity a tool_call / tool_call_update reports in its structured rawInput: codex `{ server, tool }`, cursor `{ providerIdentifier, toolName }` (both string). Never reads the title. Undefined for any other update or shape.

The own-tool decision uses this design: A turn-scoped ledger records, per `toolCallId`, the identity read from the agent's structured `rawInput` (shape chosen by `mcpCallIdentity`). A permission request whose `toolCallId` the ledger marks as the session's own (server name equals the session's, tool name is one of the session's embedder tools, no conflicting identity seen) is answered with the agent's allow-once option before the profile decision, in every profile, and recorded as an auto decision with `OWN_TOOL_REASON`. Anything else goes through the profile decision unchanged (the user ruled 2026-10-29: a card per call is acceptable when nothing matches). This story implements only the extraction and ledger portion; US-004 owns the turn lifetime and permission answer.

Failure semantics: A permission request whose `toolCallId` was never reported with structured fields → Profile decision, as today (a card under `ask`, a reject under `none`/`read`). The structured server name is not the session's (e.g. `"nax"` from a repo config) → Profile decision. The structured tool name is not one of the session's embedder tools → Profile decision. One `toolCallId` reported with two different identities → Never own; profile decision. Therefore the extractor requires nonempty strings, ignores title-only and unrelated updates, and the ledger must not let a later matching report erase conflicting identity evidence.

**Scope** — In: new src/client/own-tool-calls.ts and test/unit/client/own-tool-calls.test.ts, structured-shape discrimination for both tool update variants, exact session-server/tool membership and sticky conflicting identities. No existing modules import it yet. Out: server name generation (US-001), registry/capability changes (US-002), router attachment and permission decisions (US-004), nax delivery/audit (US-005), trusting titles or agent-visible display names.
- A size cap on the per-turn own-tool ledger; it lives for one turn only.

**Interface**
```typescript
import type { SessionUpdate } from "@agentclientprotocol/sdk";

export type McpCallIdentityKind = "codex" | "cursor";

export interface McpCallIdentity {
  readonly toolCallId: string;
  readonly server: string;
  readonly tool: string;
}

/**
 * The identity a tool_call / tool_call_update reports in its structured rawInput:
 * codex `{ server, tool }`, cursor `{ providerIdentifier, toolName }` (both string).
 * Never reads the title. Undefined for any other update or shape.
 */
export function mcpCallIdentityOf(kind: McpCallIdentityKind, update: SessionUpdate): McpCallIdentity | undefined;

export interface OwnToolLedger {
  observe(update: SessionUpdate): void;
  /** True only when every identity seen for this id names serverName and a tool in toolNames. */
  isOwn(toolCallId: string): boolean;
}

export function createOwnToolLedger(input: {
  readonly kind: McpCallIdentityKind;
  readonly serverName: string;
  readonly toolNames: readonly string[];
}): OwnToolLedger;
```
The interface comment states a necessary condition, not permission to accept multiple different identities: any two different identities for one id make it permanently non-own in this ledger, including a wrong-server report followed by the correct server. Keep this authorization evidence separate from tool-events.ts's scrubbed/capped presentation state.
